; =============================================================================
; Tunah tuner -- redundant instruction elimination
; =============================================================================
; Purpose       : Delete an instruction whose result is a value that the block
;                already holds, that is, a move of a value to itself and any
;                chain of moves and copies that can be shortened to one.
; Stage         : Instruction level machine IL, register assignment aware.
; Vocabulary    : mov and copy from the shared directory vocabulary. No
;                arithmetic, merge, memory or control operator appears here:
;                merging identical incoming values is dataflow-opt.tuner,
;                removing an operation with a neutral operand is
;                alg-sim.tuner, and deleting an instruction from a window is
;                peephole-opt.tuner. No rule body is shared with those files.
; Preconditions : (mov s d) copies s into d, so its result is the value of s.
;                A copy has no observable behaviour of its own, so a copy of a
;                copy is a copy of the original value. A self move leaves the
;                destination already holding the source value, so the move has
;                no effect and cannot be observed, even by a later partial
;                register write, because the model records whole registers.
; Cost model    : Every rule removes an instruction, so all of them win
;                extraction under the size cost model.
; Termination   : rie-mov-chain and rie-mov-of-copy replace a two-instruction
;                value with a one-instruction value, so the matched subterm
;                strictly shrinks. rie-copy-of-copy is a union of e-classes
;                that adds no node. rie-mov-self deletes its match outright.
;                No rule creates a move or a copy, so the set is monotone.
;
; Operator signatures required by this file:
; signatures: mov 2, copy 1
; =============================================================================

; A move whose source and destination are the same register has no effect, so
; the instruction is dead and its result is the value already there.
(rule rie-mov-self (mov ?d ?d) ?d)
; A move is a copy of its source; naming it as a copy lets the copy collapse
; in the e-graph and makes the moved value available to every other tuner.
(rule rie-mov-is-copy (mov ?s ?d) (copy ?s))
; Copying a copy is copying the original value, so the inner copy is redundant.
(rule rie-copy-of-copy (copy (copy ?x)) (copy ?x))
; Copying a moved value is copying the source of the move, so the move is
; redundant once its result is only a copy.
(rule rie-copy-of-mov (copy (mov ?s ?d)) (copy ?s))
; A move of a move is a move of the original source: the destination receives
; the same value with one instruction instead of two. The intermediate
; definition dies, and because the model treats a move as a whole-register
; copy there is no partial value to preserve.
(rule rie-mov-chain (mov (mov ?s ?t) ?d) (mov ?s ?d))
; A move of a copy is a move of the copied value, for the same reason.
(rule rie-mov-of-copy (mov (copy ?s) ?d) (mov ?s ?d))
